#show: main.with(
  title: "When the Score Stops Mattering",
  desc: "A ten-year forecast of AI's dominance in quantified competition, the collapse of score-based merit, and the emergence of qualitative niches.",
  date: "2026-11-18",
  tags: ("sociology-of-science", "philosophy-of-science", "ai"),
  draft: true,
)

AI will dominate quantified competition. Wherever we can specify a result, evaluate attempts, and use those evaluations to improve the next attempt, I expect machines to take over the work of getting better. This includes difficult research, clever algorithms, and improvements that once required years of specialist judgment. Difficulty can delay the process. It offers a poor claim to permanent ownership.

The larger consequence will be a collapse in the authority we give to scores. We have used measured achievement to identify expertise, assign credit, select people, and justify differences in how they are treated. When the ability to produce that achievement becomes widely available through machines, the score will say less about the person presenting it. Quantitative research will keep producing useful knowledge. The career bargain built around personally producing the better number will break.

This is a prediction about the next decade, written in November 2026. Its sharpest deadline concerns work whose evaluation and repeated trials are already affordable, or become affordable within that period. I am more confident in the technical premise than in the social conclusion. Machines may become better at the work well before institutions change how they distribute rewards. I expect these two changes to move at different speeds. The second will expose disagreements about value that common scores have allowed us to leave unresolved.

== 1. I kept trying to get away

I first encountered machine learning in 2013. Around 2015, I remember seeing systems that generated music and feeling that something fundamental had moved. I associate that memory with the early excitement around GANs, though I can no longer say with confidence which models I saw at the time. I was interested enough to approach professors about studying AI in graduate school. In the environment I encountered, people often did not take the idea seriously. I remember being called a fraud even in a class, and being treated as though I had lost my mind.

When AlphaGo defeated Lee Sedol in 2016, I was hardly surprised. #link("https://deepmind.google/research/alphago/")[\[22\]] I had already begun to worry about what would happen to work whose success could be checked. My understanding at the time was incomplete, and my career did not follow a single plan. The fear still shaped my choices. I kept looking for places where the next improvement depended on conditions that models could not yet reach.

For more than a decade, I worked close to the core of programming and systems. Understanding complicated systems, finding the mistakes that imposed large costs, and getting many parts to work together became part of my identity. Systems for AI seemed like a reasonable place to stand. More capable AI needed better infrastructure. The bottlenecks were real, the results could be measured, and expertise was hard to acquire.

When I used the GPT-3 API around 2021, that position began to feel less comfortable. I could imagine a model reading performance measurements, relating a bottleneck to the structure of the code, suggesting a change, and checking whether throughput or latency improved. My experiments at the time showed me a direction to fear rather than establishing that the entire process already worked. Once the loop became reliable, however, the very properties that made systems work satisfying would also open a path to its automation.

Looking back, building systems for AI sometimes feels like eating my own flesh. I was helping to build the machines that would make my expertise less scarce. My attachment to the craft was real. So was the pain of recognizing that a technology could become more useful while the people who worked on it became less valuable in the market. My movement toward HCI, education, fabrication, graphics, and motion has included another attempt to get out of the way. I can now describe several bets within that movement. I cannot claim that I had the whole plan from the beginning.

Much of the advice about AI starts by reassuring skilled people that their judgment will always matter. I find it difficult to start there. The part of someone's judgment that can consistently be expressed as a better outcome is precisely the part that a learning system has a path toward acquiring. The years spent developing it can make the loss more painful. They place no obligation on a machine to leave it alone.

== 2. A score gives a machine somewhere to go

Consider an engineer trying to make a program faster. She examines the code, forms a hypothesis, changes something, runs a test, and compares the results. Her expertise helps her decide which changes are worth attempting and which measurements can be trusted. An agent that can participate in this loop acquires more than the ability to write code. It acquires a way to discover that its first answer was wrong, retain useful changes, and spend more effort on promising directions.

AlphaEvolve makes this concrete. Its developers report using an evolutionary coding agent to improve a selection rule inside Google's Borg scheduler, recovering an average of 0.7 percent of fleet-wide compute resources. They also report improvements to kernels used in Gemini training. These are developer-reported results for particular components, with the surrounding systems in place. For someone who has worked on Systems for AI, the implication is direct. The machinery used to train the models has itself entered the models' search space. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2506.13131v1")[\[1\]]

In robotics, the same pattern appears one step earlier in the process. Researchers spend considerable effort designing rewards that teach a robot what to do. Eureka used a language model to write reward code, trained policies in simulation, read the results, and revised the rewards. Its authors report outperforming expert-designed rewards on 83 percent of 29 simulated tasks. A specialist's method for producing good behavior had become something the system could search over. #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12931v2")[\[2\]]

Moving one level upward offers little automatic protection. DiscoRL discovered learning rules through meta-learning, including rules that transferred beyond the environments used to discover them. It is a different kind of system from an LLM coding agent, but it reaches into the same professional territory: developing the methods through which improvement takes place. A researcher who says humans will always design the learning algorithms is laying claim to something already being optimized. #link("https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09761-x")[\[3\]]

Even the cost of checking an answer can change. In work on the computational hardness of approximation problems, researchers used AlphaEvolve to search for mathematical constructions and improve the code that verified them. They report roughly a ten-thousand-fold acceleration for checking particular final constructions, which they subsequently checked with independent exhaustive procedures. The result concerns those constructions and verification procedures. It shows that a barrier to automated search can itself become a subject of automated search. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18057v7")[\[4\]]

There are several conditions here. Giving something a number is only the beginning. There must be something we can actually evaluate. The evaluator must be reliable enough to withstand attempts to exploit it. Useful candidates must be reachable, and trials must be affordable. Assigning a score to a problem establishes none of those conditions by itself. Physical experiments can be slow, destructive, or difficult to reset. A convenient simulator may reward behavior that fails in reality. There is no theorem saying that recursive improvement makes every measurable problem easy.

My forecast is that these conditions will be satisfied for a much wider range of work. Better models, search procedures, simulators, sensors, and robots will make more of the loop possible. Some tasks will take much longer than others. For the tasks that become reachable, machines only need to do better than people at an affordable cost. They can displace specialists long before finding a global optimum.

This extends beyond incremental engineering. Large improvements, surprising mathematical constructions, and difficult proofs can emerge from processes whose results can be checked. The magnitude of a discovery offers no reservation of its authorship for humans. Moving to problem formulation also provides little permanent refuge. Selecting experiments, building evaluators, and some of the work of choosing questions can enter the loop. A claim of protection needs an account of what the system cannot reach and why.

== 3. Useful results, disappearing expertise

A research result can retain its scientific value while the person needed to produce it becomes less scarce. This distinction matters for understanding the collapse I expect. A faster kernel still saves time. A better mathematical construction still changes what is known. The labor required to obtain a result, the price paid for that labor, the credit given to the person presenting it, and the prestige of the venue publishing it can move in different directions.

Imagine a small software company comparing two developers. One spent ten years learning to diagnose performance problems. The other can now use an agent that finds and repairs the same problems. If the systems they deliver perform equally well, the company has less reason to pay for the history of effort behind the first developer's answer. The experienced developer may have other abilities. Those abilities now need to justify the difference. The old performance advantage can no longer do that work.

There is limited experimental evidence for one step in this process. Noy and Zhang studied 453 professionals completing short writing tasks. Access to ChatGPT improved the assessed quality of their work and narrowed differences between participants. This was a study of bounded writing tasks rather than a study of careers or social standing. Still, it illustrates how an output score can lose some of its ability to distinguish individual producers. #link("https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adh2586")[\[5\]]

Research has always assigned credit through tools, laboratories, and collaboration. The AI-discovery papers I have just cited still confer distinction on their human authors. That arrangement can continue while a researcher's decisions make the result difficult for others to reproduce with comparable resources. My forecast begins to bite when available agents repeatedly produce the relevant improvements without that person's unusual contribution. At that point, a better benchmark result mainly records a reproducible capability or a resource advantage. Reliable delivery can remain valuable engineering, and the discovery can remain useful science, while the number loses its former force as evidence of exceptional personal insight. Institutions may continue awarding prestige anyway. Their willingness to revise that practice is the social change I am predicting.

Ranked competition can always become more difficult. If everyone scores 90, the examiner can make the questions harder. New performance differences can also emerge between teams of humans and AI. My bet is that automated improvement will erode existing individual advantages faster than institutions can develop equally broad measures of individual ability and merit. If new common scores reliably distinguish exceptional human contributions under comparable resource conditions, the old arrangement will have found a successor. I would take that outcome as evidence against the collapse I expect.

Chess is an obvious and important objection. Human tournaments, ratings, prizes, and admiration have survived machines becoming better players. FIDE restricts outside assistance, and the Candidates Tournament still uses scores to select a human challenger. #link("https://handbook.fide.com/chapter/E012023")[\[6\]] Society can value human performance in its own right and preserve it. Exams, degrees, and professional qualifications can also maintain boundaries around permitted tools.

The difference lies in what an institution is trying to buy. A chess tournament offers a contest between people under agreed constraints. A data center purchases useful scheduling. Its operator has a direct reason to adopt the better method. Research combines two purposes: discovering useful knowledge and distributing standing among researchers. As those purposes separate, institutions will need to explain why they retain human-only competition. The existence of a score will become a weaker justification for the broader privileges attached to winning.

I expect the separation to appear first in work where tools are permitted and results are easy to check. Hiring and paper reviewing will then face questions of attribution. What exactly does this result tell us about this applicant or author? Admissions and professional qualifications may move more slowly because they allocate scarce opportunities and have institutional backing. Their continued existence would hardly surprise me. What I expect to weaken is their ability to make a high rank look like a person's general superiority.

Ownership can push in the opposite direction. If the strongest models, datasets, and experimental facilities remain concentrated, competition for better numbers may become fierce. Scores may become clearer records of who controls compute and deployment. Common capabilities can become cheap while the frontier remains expensive. Such a future would weaken the merit-based explanation of rewards without distributing power more evenly. The person whose skill loses scarcity may also lack ownership of the system that replaced it.

This is the social risk facing the many people who have made a place for themselves through education, practice, and reliable work. They may lose bargaining power before alternative institutions are ready. Selection will continue. As common performance stops distinguishing people, capital, access, trust, relationships, visibility, and cultural standing may gain influence. The collapse of a quantitative value system could leave behind a more openly unequal society.

== 4. What we have put inside objective numbers

Here I use objectivity to mean a representation that several subjective judgments have stabilized within a shared system of communication. It is collective subjectivity organized through procedures. The way it is organized matters. Someone chooses the categories, questions, weights, admissible evidence, and whose judgment counts. A common representation can become reliable while retaining the history of those choices.

School examinations illustrate the process. Korea's College Scholastic Ability Test turns performance on selected tasks under specified conditions into comparable scores. The answers can be checked consistently. Using those answers to distribute educational opportunities involves broader judgments about what students should know and what a score should entitle them to receive. Work on measurement and fairness describes the gap between a concept we care about and the procedure used to measure it. #link("https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/measurement-and-fairness/")[\[7\]] My claim about the social authority of objectivity goes further and is a philosophical position.

Physical constraints remain. A bridge can collapse despite unanimous approval, and a robot can hit a wall despite excellent user ratings. Shared measurement helps us give reliable accounts of those events. A further step selects which events matter, combines them, and ranks entire activities or people using the result. The world constrains our measurements. It supplies no single ordering of all the goods we might pursue.

Language performs a related compression. I think of language as a minimal representation for explaining the world to one another. By minimal, I mean that communication carries a selected account of an experience in place of reproducing the experience itself. I use the word philosophically, without making a claim about mathematically shortest descriptions. Saying that a robot moved gently can be enough for a conversation. How the movement felt, to whom, and under what circumstances remain open.

I make a stronger claim about qualities of experience that cannot be translated completely into common sentences or scores. I mean more than the ordinary difference between a description and its object. My commitment is that no shared description can substitute for the first-person quality of the experience, even when it predicts a person's response accurately. People can understand the same words while finding the movement reassuring, irritating, intimate, or threatening. Language can point, compare, evoke, and help us notice. Fully transmitting the quality of the experience asks for something else. This is a philosophical position about experience, stronger than anything a study of preferences can establish.

Recording more numbers does not resolve that demand. A motion has coordinates, velocity, acceleration, and force, and a video can be stored as numerical data. A participant can give it four out of five. These are records of a physical event and of a judgment about the event. Treating those records as a common scale containing the whole experience requires another argument. A representation can be extremely useful without settling that question.

When a common scale loses its authority to determine value, the differences compressed into it become harder to dismiss as personal matters. I expect those differences to become organizing principles for research and production. People will encounter many things that work extraordinarily well by shared standards and continue to disagree about which of those things they want to live with. The disagreement will survive another decimal place of performance.

== 5. Speaking, seeing, remembering, acting

I think we discovered a remarkably powerful system for working with language somewhat unexpectedly. Attention was deliberately designed, and the Transformer paper proposed a specific architecture. The eventual reach of the systems developed from that line of work exceeded some of the researchers' expectations. Their recollections differ in how much they foresaw. #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762")[\[8\]] #link("https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/what-is-a-transformer-model/")[\[9\]] Calling this the discovery of a linguistic cognitive system is my interpretation of its significance. It makes no claim that these systems think in the same way as humans.

Fluent language can make it tempting to assume that the rest of perception has been solved to a similar degree. A paper titled "Vision language models are blind" challenged this assumption using simple visual tasks. Models from the period studied could struggle with counts and spatial details that people read directly from images. The authors also found cases where useful information existed in visual features but the language component failed to turn it into the right answer. #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06581")[\[10\]] Recognizing what an image is about and reading its internal relationships precisely were different achievements.

The result has a date. The subsequent project "Vision Language Models Are Biased" reports substantial improvement in its October 2026 evaluation of GPT-6 Astra, alongside remaining failures on modified familiar images. #link("https://vlmsarebiased.github.io/")[\[11\]] "Basically blind" can describe a particular failure to use the visual evidence available. It is a poor permanent diagnosis for every system called a VLM. The boundary is already moving. A research bet resting on one old benchmark result can lose its force quickly.

Several abilities also need separating. Detecting a cup, counting cups, and determining which cup is behind another ask different questions. Following the same cup through a video adds identity over time. SAM 3 combines detection with memory-based tracking to maintain object identities across frames. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16719v2")[\[23\]] Remembering where a cup went after it disappears requires stored state and later retrieval. Generating a convincing scene adds another task: VBench-2.0 evaluates physical behavior and the preservation of individual objects separately from visual polish. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2503.21755v2")[\[24\]] These results establish specific capabilities. A robot must connect those capabilities to action, then update its understanding when an action changes the world.

Physical Intelligence's work on embodied memory illustrates this last problem and a way to improve it. In one controlled task, a robot sees an object put into one of four drawers and must later retrieve it. A policy without memory performs at chance level. The proposed system combines recent visual history with longer-term language memory, reporting improvements on memory tasks and multi-stage work lasting up to fifteen minutes. #link("https://www.pi.website/research/memory")[\[12\]] The four drawers describe the experiment. They do not establish a universal limit on the number of objects a model can remember.

There is a complication here for my own argument. Compression into text can help a robot retain information it needs. Visual systems also commonly use learned visual representations, so an obligatory conversion of images into sentences cannot explain every failure. Resolution can erase detail, and compression can discard distinctions. Prior knowledge can override visible evidence. Memory can become stale. An action may be needed to discover that the current estimate of the world was wrong. Which explanation applies must be established for the system and task in question. #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06581")[\[10\]] #link("https://vlmsarebiased.github.io/")[\[11\]] #link("https://www.pi.website/research/memory")[\[12\]]

A related distinction applies to sound. A transcript can preserve the words of a response while losing the timing that made it sound impatient. An audio model can preserve and learn more of that timing. The listener's history and the surrounding relationship still shape the experience. The existence of qualities that escape verbal description gives us reason to study sound and interaction directly. It does not establish that a machine can never learn from them.

A system with robust visual memory, reliable action, and effective learning of individual preferences would overturn some of my current bets. I expect substantial advances in those directions. A harder distinction remains. Learning to produce what a person likes and establishing a common score that fully captures why it matters are different achievements. The first can advance greatly while the second continues to fail.

== 6. The differences we used to call personal

A robotic guide gives us a concrete view of this problem. In a study with 18 blind and low-vision participants, most preferred quieter walking, while some wanted audible cues to understand the robot's location or operating state. Participants also experienced the robot's resistance to their movement differently. A firmer gait could inspire confidence, and greater compliance could give a sense of control. The study used a Wizard-of-Oz setup, with people supplying some of the apparent autonomy, and combined numerical ratings with qualitative feedback. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2501.04594v1")[\[13\]]

Imagine an engineering team making the robot quieter and raising its average comfort score. That may be a useful result. It does not follow that the quieter robot is also better for someone who relied on its sound. Adding more users to an average can produce a more accurate estimate of the majority preference while leaving the disagreement in place. Weights can help us make a design decision by giving certain interests priority. They do not dissolve the conflicting experiences.

The same issue appears when the tone I like in an assistant is unbearable to you. Sometimes a new user or setting falls outside the training distribution. Sometimes the relevant people were already represented and their differences were lost through averaging. Sometimes people disagree because they want incompatible forms of treatment. Calling all three situations out of distribution hides the choices already made about whose experience the system should represent.

I expect qualitative research to expand here. Observation, interviews, participation, and direct experience can reveal differences that an existing questionnaire never asked about. Soma design, for instance, treats bodily experience as part of discovering and developing design questions. #link("https://doi.org/10.1145/3462448")[\[14\]] Researchers can make something, experience it with others, revise their understanding, and preserve accounts that do not agree. The categories of inquiry can change along with the things being made.

Human participation alone does not make a study qualitative. Preference votes and comfort ratings can be analyzed quantitatively, and often are. A vivid demonstration alone does not establish a claim about experience either. Qualitative research gains its strength by making observations, interpretations, contexts, and disagreements open to examination. It can show why a difference matters without claiming to have produced a ranking that every observer should accept.

AI will enter this work too. Preference learning already trains systems through comparisons between behaviors. #link("https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2017/hash/d5e2c0adad503c91f91df240d0cd4e49-Abstract.html")[\[15\]] Agents can help build prototypes, analyze records, and learn how particular people respond. Some procedures that we currently call qualitative research will become easier to automate. A system may learn to anticipate a person's responses from video, sound, touch, and action even where the experience cannot be fully translated into words. Human exclusivity would require a separate argument, and I see no reason to grant it in general.

I still expect a great expansion of niches as making variants becomes cheaper and common performance loses its power to impose a single direction. Some niches will expose differences that were already present. Others will arise because new things make unfamiliar experiences possible. A particular way of moving together, a visual style shared by a small community, or a learning environment built around an unusual social practice can find a place among people without winning a universal competition.

Platforms could also steer much of this production in similar directions. One experiment found that AI assistance improved the ratings of individual short stories while making the collection of stories more alike. #link("https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11244532/")[\[21\]] Cheap variation and actual diversity are different outcomes. Distribution, defaults, ownership, and the work required to reach a community will help determine which outcome we get. A culturally diverse world may still offer very little paid work to the people who contribute to it.

== 7. Six bets with different ways to fail

I can now read my path as a series of bets about these boundaries, with both the benefit and the distortion of hindsight. Systems for AI was a bet on the value of infrastructure. The work taught me how directly a field organized around throughput, latency, and efficiency invites automated improvement. It has also been painful to watch the professional identity I gained from that work erode. If specialists with comparable tools continue to outperform automated search consistently across important systems problems, I will need to revise my expectations about the speed and extent of that erosion.

HCI is a bet on the fact that introducing a technology changes the situation in which people understand it. A design can change whose voice is heard, who has control, and what interactions people consider acceptable. Research questions can arise through working with people and observing the consequences. Soma design supplies a methodological example, and the guide-robot study shows how reasonable users can want opposite things. This bet weakens if systems can discover such disagreements, sustain participation, and revise practices with little reliance on human researchers. Adding an interview to a benchmark paper offers little protection.

The fabrication bet is different. A physical prototype brings people into contact with materials, objects, and possible actions that a description may fail to reach. Imagine someone making a handle in a community workshop and discovering that the shapes people can grasp with confidence also change what they want to make. This is an example of a possible research situation, rather than an event from my past. Precision, yield, cost, and any grip force we can specify remain targets of automation. The bet concerns what becomes apparent while making and using something in a particular place. If inexpensive autonomous fabrication preserves that situated participation while removing most of the need for specialists to mediate it, the role I presently expect for researchers will shrink.

Graphics is a bet on making phenomena people can see and explore. I like work such as RoSE4D, which models changes like a flower opening and withering, for the visual worlds it opens up. The paper uses CLIP-based evaluation and a participant preference study. It belongs to a research culture that measures results. #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2412.05278v2")[\[16\]] My interest lies in the new object of experience that remains after the comparisons are finished. If highly capable generators make that phenomenon and all its meaningful variants commonplace, the technical contribution will become less scarce. The next contribution will need to lie in new scenes and questions.

Motion and HRI involve a more immediate encounter. The timing of an approach, the force of a pull, and the moment of release are experienced through a body in a relationship. A perfectly repeatable trajectory may be welcome in one encounter and intrusive in another. The guide-robot findings illustrate this possibility. The bet concerns discovering and negotiating such conditions through interaction. It weakens as robots learn them better from each person and adapt without specialist help. Reliable physical control itself remains squarely within the competition I expect AI to dominate.

Education adds an institutional condition that the other bets do not require. My explicit forecast is that, even when AI and robots are commonplace, human teachers will remain the principal figures responsible for sustained classroom relationships and educational judgment in primary and secondary schools. I expect a child to have a human classroom teacher who knows them and acts within that relationship. Someone who merely approves decisions made elsewhere would not satisfy this prediction.

The rationale begins with what schools organize. They bring children into relationships with adults and peers, give them responsibilities, deal with conflict, and help them find a place in a shared world. Biesta describes educational purposes in terms of qualification, socialization, and subjectification. #link("https://dspace.stir.ac.uk/handle/1893/1612")[\[17\]] This is an account of the purposes of education. It helps explain why the substantial question of who should educate a child remains open after the problem of providing a correct explanation has been solved.

AI may become extraordinarily good at tutoring. A randomized crossover study of 194 undergraduate physics students reported better immediate learning outcomes in less time with a carefully designed AI tutor than in the comparison classroom lessons. #link("https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12179260/")[\[18\]] The study concerns particular lessons and learners. I take it seriously as evidence for improvements in tutoring. Sustained relationships and responsibilities in schools require separate evidence.

Current policy gives some reason to expect human agency to remain part of that arrangement. UNESCO's AI competency framework for teachers treats human agency as a significant consideration in how teachers work with AI. #link("https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-teachers")[\[19\]] This documents a present institutional direction. My further inference is that families and schools will continue to value human adults who can account for how they have treated a child, participate in a community, and maintain a relationship across situations. Such responsibility has a social form as well as a functional description.

There are powerful pressures against this bet. Teacher shortages, costs, and unequal access make automated instruction attractive. #link("https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/global-report-teachers-addressing-teacher-shortages-and-transforming-profession")[\[20\]] Schools may keep far fewer human staff. Wealthier families may purchase sustained human relationships while others receive a mostly automated service. Parents and children may come to prefer AI-centered schools. If human teachers are reduced to nominal supervisors of very large numbers of students, or if sustained human educational judgment becomes a luxury, the prediction will have failed in an important respect. The presence of a human name on an organizational chart cannot save it.

These moves share a cost. I have left places where I understood how to work while remaining attached to the expertise that had given me a place there. Systems for AI added the pain of contributing to the loss of its scarcity. My later bets involve different conditions. A temporary gap in visual memory, a discovery through a prototype, and a community's decision about its teachers can each change in different ways. Grouping them together as safe human work would obscure the reasons for choosing them. The boundaries that prompted a move can continue to move after I arrive.

== 8. What should become visible by 2036

By 2029, I expect automated search to become an ordinary baseline in areas such as kernel optimization, scheduling heuristics, and reward design in simulation. Improvements obtained through manual design will increasingly need to explain why readily available agents could not achieve comparable results. Evidence against this forecast would be sustained human superiority across a broad range of these tasks after comparable access, compute, and opportunities for evaluation are provided. Occasional human wins can coexist with machine dominance. A persistent general advantage would make the prediction difficult to maintain.

By 2032, I expect tool-permitted software hiring and benchmark-driven research evaluation to give substantially less authority to a submitted performance score as evidence of exceptional individual merit. I would look for changed hiring and review criteria, a reduced advantage from the score when access to tools is disclosed, and less willingness among evaluators to treat it as a warrant for wider professional status. Responsibility and consequences in use should carry more of the decision. This prediction requires changes in selection as well as rhetoric. If common scores retain their present weight across repeated cohorts after strong tools become broadly accessible, the institutional part of my forecast has failed, even if the automation premise is correct.

Over the next decade, I expect common performance to enter the infrastructure and research and production centered on particular people, bodies, materials, places, and shared practices to become more visible. Qualitative inquiry will help shape these activities and the questions they raise, often in collaboration with AI. Some of its procedures will enter automation as they become repeatable. The boundary will continue to shift with what can be represented, evaluated, and supplied cheaply. Getting away from numbers will therefore involve repeated moves, without a final protected destination.

A competing future could preserve all these differences while automating much of the work of discovering and responding to them. Systems might learn changing preferences separately for each person, produce the corresponding artifacts, and carry out much of the contextual inquiry. Qualitative activity could then expand while human researchers lost ground within it. I would count that as evidence against any professional refuge implied by these bets. A different failure would occur if familiar common rankings continued to organize these activities and their participants accepted them as adequate accounts of value. Meanwhile, if scores kept deciding opportunities through inherited power, the institutional collapse I predicted would still require observable changes in acceptance and selection. An unchanged system cannot be rescued for this forecast by calling it secretly hollow.

For people outside the small group that owns frontier models and infrastructure, the remaining space may be considerably narrower than familiar advice suggests. Studying harder, polishing a skill, and learning another tool may buy time while the same machinery approaches the next target. A niche can be deeply meaningful and offer no salary. Human relationships can be cherished while institutions that pay for them have their budgets cut. An economy has no obligation to replace positions whose reasons for existing it has undermined.

The future I expect contains abundant measurable achievement and the collapse of its authority as a general certificate of human worth. The research bargain that turns a better shared score into evidence of exceptional personal merit will break. A wider range of experiences, communities, and incompatible values will become visible, along with harsher conflicts over ownership and access. Scores will continue to organize machines and bounded contests. The social order that asked people to earn their place by becoming the better-scoring machine will lose its claim on them.

